 * active" and "this certificate was renewed".
 *
 * Two incidents from the 2026-07-26 rehearsal's M7 list live here.
 *
 * M7 #6 — *"SSL certificate renewed for example.com — Verified live … valid
 * until <old date>"*. `certbot certonly` exits 0 and prints "Certificate not yet
 * due for renewal" when it does NOTHING, and that string was in the same
 * success-marker alternation as "Congratulations". The unchanged expiry was then
 * read back off disk and reported as a renewal. Nothing had been renewed.
 * `classifyCertbotOutcome` separates the two, because an exit code is not proof.
 *
 * M7 #5 — the served-certificate probe checked only name-match and a future
 * `notAfter`. Behind a CDN that terminates TLS at its edge (Cloudflare, Fastly),
 * the probe captures the EDGE certificate — which is valid, publicly trusted and
 * covers the domain — and the sweep then overwrote `expiresAt` with the edge's
 * date. The origin's real certificate was therefore never seen to be expiring,
 * never renewed, and died silently. `servedCertVerdict` refuses to adopt an
 * expiry from a certificate it can positively see is not ours, and it stops
 * treating an untrusted certificate as ACTIVE.
 *
 * The discriminator is the ISSUER, deliberately, and only in one direction: a
 * later `notAfter` from OUR issuer is certbot's own systemd timer renewing
 * out-of-band, which we want to pick up. A certificate from someone else's
 * issuer is not ours to reason about, so we keep our own record and say why.
 * Where the issuer cannot be judged we adopt nothing and invent nothing.
 *
 * Deterministic: no TLS, no SSH, no clock. Covered by test/ssl-verify.test.ts.
 */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Did certbot actually issue anything?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export type CertbotOutcome =
  | { kind: "issued"; detail: string }
  | { kind: "unchanged"; detail: string }
  | { kind: "failed"; detail: string };

/** certbot printed one of these only when it really obtained a certificate. */
const ISSUED_MARKERS = /Congratulations|Successfully received certificate|Certificate is saved/i;
/** certbot printed this when it decided there was nothing to do. */
const UNCHANGED_MARKERS = /not yet due for renewal|Certificate not yet due|keeping the existing certificate/i;

/**
 * Classify a certbot run. The exit code alone is worthless here: certbot exits 0
 * both when it issues and when it declines to.
 */
export function classifyCertbotOutcome(code: number, output: string): CertbotOutcome {
  const text = String(output ?? "");
  const detail = text.trim().slice(0, 400);
  if (code !== 0) return { kind: "failed", detail };
  // Order matters: a forced renewal prints BOTH strings, and in that case a
  // certificate really was issued.
  if (ISSUED_MARKERS.test(text)) return { kind: "issued", detail };
  if (UNCHANGED_MARKERS.test(text)) return { kind: "unchanged", detail };
  return { kind: "failed", detail };
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Whose certificate is on the wire?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** What the TLS handshake told us about the certificate being served. */
export type ServedIdentity = {
  notAfter: Date;
  coversDomain: boolean;
  /** Issuer common name, e.g. "R11", "E5", "WE1". "" when unreadable. */
  issuerCN: string;
  /** Issuer organisation, e.g. "Let's Encrypt", "Google Trust Services". "" when unreadable. */
  issuerO: string;
  /** `socket.authorized` — would a browser trust this chain? */
  authorized: boolean;
  /** `socket.authorizationError`, "" when authorized. */
  authorizationError: string;
};

